00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:20.300
First, supply
chain security 101.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:23.670
When we talk about supply
chain, the most common type

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.730
of supply chain is
food supply chain.

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:30.690
Crops are planted,
harvested, milled

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:35.470
into fours, baked into bread,
delivered to our dinner table.

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:37.340
And then we ask questions
about our food--

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:41.340
is it organic, where did does
come from, is it FDA approved?

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:46.620
Very similarly, code go through
a similar type of cycle.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.890
Developers write code,
code get checked in,

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:54.480
get baked into images, goes
through tests and verification,

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:58.680
and go through quality controls,
then deployed to our production

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:02.580
environment-- having access
to super sensitive security

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.090
resources.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.450
And then we have security
stakeholders and compliance

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:10.340
officers asking questions--
where does this code come from,

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:12.810
is it vulnerability-free,
does it

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:17.220
need my compliance requirement,
and most importantly,

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:18.720
with all the
requirement that I have

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.330
to meet, how do I
meet all of them

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:26.020
without losing speed
of my development?

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:30.560
So traditionally for
VM-based workloads,

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:34.490
many customers run big,
monolithic applications that

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:37.340
gets released only
a few times a year

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:40.850
due to its scale and
the complexity involved

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:43.940
to release it.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:46.970
For each release, security
and compliance stakeholders

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:50.900
have to manually inspect
every component of the release

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:55.340
to make sure that it's
approved and it's up to date,

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:58.300
it's vulnerability-free.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:01.340
And because VM is
a opaque format,

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:05.960
this process is cumbersome
to say the least.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:08.240
And, eventually, when
the code is finally

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.120
released to production,
a number of people

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.400
needs access to the
running applications

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:16.670
fairly regularly to
apply ongoing patches,

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:19.320
ongoing maintenance,
and fine tuning.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:24.140
So this is a very
manual process.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:28.010
And many different roles
in the organization

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:31.490
have direct access to
the running application

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:34.370
because they need
to react to problems

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:36.060
discovered during runtime.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:39.620
So it's a very reactive
security model as well.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.170
So it's a process that we're
probably all familiar with,

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:44.390
but necessarily love.

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:47.060
How does container
change things up?

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.942
So Juan is going to
tell us how that works.

00:03:49.942 --> 00:03:51.650
JUAN SEBASTIAN OVIEDO:
Thank you, Sandra.

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:53.340
Hello everyone.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:56.600
So as we've seen with
VMs, typically, there

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:59.600
are many manual steps
that sometimes take

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:01.860
a significant amount of time.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:03.980
This is manageable
because of the number

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:07.760
of components involved and
the time between releases.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:10.310
Those two variables are
very different in the world

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:11.990
of containers.

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:13.880
Microservices
multiply the number

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:17.180
of jobs an enterprise
can run in production

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:20.300
and current CI/CD systems
enable teams to have

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:22.640
multiple deployments a day.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:24.650
Ensuring that the
proper security

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:26.720
checks are performed in
each of these deployments

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:28.910
is very challenging.

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:31.430
Historically, the
process of restricting

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:34.700
what can be deployed has
relied on human trust,

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:37.010
and operational knowledge,
and that doesn't really

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:41.690
scale to a volume of deployments
that we see with containers.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:45.683
So now that we know about some
of these challenges provided

00:04:45.683 --> 00:04:47.600
by containers, what are
some of the properties

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.930
that they offer that we can
use to help us apply security

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:53.800
while providing velocity?

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:56.420
Containers have some
unique properties.

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:58.700
First, they are
declarative, which

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:03.050
means that as you build them,
the set of libraries, versions,

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:05.790
and components are
clearly laid out.

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:09.460
This also means that because
of the declarative nature,

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:11.930
they're also a statically
inspectable, which

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:14.690
means that you can scan them for
known security vulnerabilities

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:17.250
as soon as they are
built without needing

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.850
to run the container image.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:22.940
And other key property is
that they are immutable,

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:26.330
because they are uniquely
identified by a digest

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:28.970
that, once created,
can't be altered.

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:31.580
This enables you to track
a particular container

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:34.860
as it moves through your
solver supply chain.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:37.680
So given these properties,
what are some steps

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:39.390
that we can take
to apply security

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:44.460
while enabling our teams to
create and move with velocity?

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:47.280
First, we can build from
trusted sources, which

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:50.190
means using a set
of pre-approved

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:54.310
and regularly patched and
maintained base images.

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:57.810
These images should also have
the minimal set of dependencies

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:00.450
that are needed by
your service, and it's

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:01.950
something that
should be controlled

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:05.970
by a-- have a common base
for your organization.

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:08.650
Also, you should have
streamlined security check

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:10.380
points as part of
your release process.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:12.810
This means applying
vulnerability scanning

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:17.360
and other security-related
test as your artifacts

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:19.320
move through a supply chain.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:21.750
And you should also
define policies

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:25.402
that enable you to
apply consistent rules

00:06:25.402 --> 00:06:26.985
across different
deployments performed

00:06:26.985 --> 00:06:29.867
by the different teams
in your organization.

00:06:29.867 --> 00:06:31.950
So, as we've seen, containers
have some properties

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:34.050
that present challenges,
but also create

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:37.180
opportunities to apply security
while maintaining velocity.

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:39.450
Now, I'm going to hand
it over to Rakesh,

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:43.190
so he can tell us how ANZ
is handling this space.

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:44.330
RAKESH GARALA: Thank you.

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:46.290
Morning, everybody.

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:50.190
So before we talk
about what ANZ's doing,

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:52.460
let's just explain
