00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:13.930
on how you classify that.

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:15.680
So I just want to give
you a bit of a view

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:17.780
of the size of our
enterprise's scale

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:20.510
of what we're trying to
do, and how big we are,

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:22.230
and where we're based out of.

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:26.420
So if you look at
what NWOW is, there

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:28.310
are two key things
that we identified.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:32.480
So one was the technology
landscape was changing.

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:35.690
And what that opens up to
was a different competitive

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:36.620
landscape.

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:42.830
We found that within
Australia FinTechs [? new ?]

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:46.460
banks are really starting to
actually disrupt the market.

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:48.860
But what we also found
was our customers

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:50.060
had a different expectation.

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:53.070
So in a digital world where
technology has actually evolved

00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:55.400
and we're able to offer
digital capabilities

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.765
and digital services,
we found our customers

00:07:57.765 --> 00:07:59.390
wanted something very
different from us

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:02.270
as a banking institution.

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:04.340
So we knew we needed to adapt.

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:06.170
And what we decided was--

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:08.820
we were already building
great customer experiences,

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:12.200
but we felt that we needed to
do is build them much faster

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:13.790
and build them at scale.

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:16.670
And NWOW, or New
Ways of Working,

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:19.600
is our way of basically
trying to achieve that.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:23.510
So NWOW is a organizational
transformation.

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:26.300
It starts with us
reorganizing ourselves

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:28.550
across [INAUDIBLE]
[? tribes ?] domain,

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:30.200
so actually we're
going to redesign

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:33.380
and we have been redesigning
our organization.

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:36.432
So you can see there
our CEO, basically,

00:08:36.432 --> 00:08:38.140
said he wanted to blow
up our bureaucracy

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:40.309
and actually get the
organization much more

00:08:40.309 --> 00:08:43.353
customer-centric,
and product focused.

00:08:43.353 --> 00:08:44.270
It doesn't stop there.

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:47.030
We're also streamlining a lot
of our organizational processes

00:08:47.030 --> 00:08:49.017
and our governance approaches.

00:08:49.017 --> 00:08:51.350
So what we wanted to do was
strip and streamline as many

00:08:51.350 --> 00:08:54.650
of them as possible to allow
our employees to actually focus

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:57.620
on the consumer and embrace
agility to deliver value

00:08:57.620 --> 00:09:00.230
to the consumer faster.

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:03.050
Third and finally, and
probably most importantly,

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:05.662
technology was something
we want to tackle

00:09:05.662 --> 00:09:06.620
and have been tackling.

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:10.220
So we know as an organization
that is over 180 years

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:13.070
old, as part of this
transformation, our technology

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:15.830
landscape had to also
evolve and change to shift--

00:09:15.830 --> 00:09:18.350
to make advantage of the product
centricity and the agility

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:20.810
we're trying to embed with
some of our organizational and

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:23.510
process redesign.

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:26.010
So what are we actually doing
from a technology perspective?

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:27.580
We're doing a whole
range of things.

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:29.122
I'm not going to
talk to all of them.

00:09:29.122 --> 00:09:32.080
There's probably two key things
that I want to highlight.

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:34.390
So one is, we want to
continue our journey

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:36.920
with containerization, which
we started several years ago.

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:40.180
So we felt then and we still
believe that containerization

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:42.820
gives us pace, it gives
us greater stability,

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:44.790
but, probably, most
importantly, it

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:49.300
allows us to build good
technology with good practices.

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:51.550
As part of that, though,
what we've also identified

00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:53.300
is another opportunity.

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:56.030
So what we started to identify
across the organization

00:09:56.030 --> 00:09:58.510
and what you see on the left
hand side the picture is--

00:09:58.510 --> 00:10:00.437
we have very bespoke
release practices--

00:10:00.437 --> 00:10:02.770
some of that related to the
things the guys have already

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:05.890
talked about, but some
of that related to--

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:08.050
as teams started to
embrace containerization,

00:10:08.050 --> 00:10:09.758
they started to develop
their own release

00:10:09.758 --> 00:10:11.710
practices, and their
own automation,

00:10:11.710 --> 00:10:13.330
in pockets and silos.

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:15.790
So what we wanted to do was
actually give the organization

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:18.310
and give our engineers
a way to develop,

00:10:18.310 --> 00:10:21.070
engineer, and actually
push code to customers

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:23.410
in an industrialized
way utilizing

00:10:23.410 --> 00:10:26.150
an industrialized CI/CD
capability, which we're calling

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:29.140
the paved roads or, as we've
referred to here already,

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:30.800
is our supply chain.

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:34.420
So the idea of this is that
it doesn't just only help

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:38.080
our engineers, but it also
helps our customers get access

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:41.370
to our products, and features,
and value add a lot earlier.

00:10:44.590 --> 00:10:48.500
ANZ is a great place to work
for a whole range of reasons.

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:51.052
And I say that as
an ANZ employee,

00:10:51.052 --> 00:10:52.510
but one of the key
things for us is

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:53.860
that we're a
continually evolving

00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:55.027
and a learning organization.

00:10:55.027 --> 00:10:58.120
So we don't like to
rest on ideas that we're

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:00.860
starting to develop and push,
but we look for opportunities.

00:11:00.860 --> 00:11:03.770
And that's inbred in
our culture and our DNA.

00:11:03.770 --> 00:11:06.490
So as we started to
progress on these roads,

00:11:06.490 --> 00:11:09.910
we started to ask ourselves
some open questions to ourselves

00:11:09.910 --> 00:11:12.880
and said, what we're doing
is good, but it's not great.

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:16.150
So what we're doing opens
us up for speed in pockets,

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:17.470
but then how do we scale that?

00:11:17.470 --> 00:11:19.303
So the guys have already
talked a little bit

00:11:19.303 --> 00:11:21.950
about containerization
gives of scaling challenges.

00:11:21.950 --> 00:11:23.770
So we started to
acknowledge what can we

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:25.270
do to actually
scale the work we're

00:11:25.270 --> 00:11:29.010
doing across the entire
breadth of our enterprise.

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:30.760
It also then opens up
to actually thinking

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:32.230
about your
operational processes.

00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:36.650
How can we govern, run, operate,
and think about security

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:37.150
better?

00:11:37.150 --> 00:11:39.610
And how do we actually
start to embed that

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:41.790
into our organization?

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:44.400
And then as we
progress past that,

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:46.650
we started to think about
how we automate governance.

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:48.990
So can we automate
a lot of the things

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:51.300
that we would normally do
as part of a supply chain

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:52.230
manually?

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:55.400
Can we automate that using
some of these capabilities

00:11:55.400 --> 00:11:58.180
or using some of the new
technologies available to us

00:11:58.180 --> 00:12:00.840
to make that even
more streamlined?

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:03.300
And the final bit for us was--

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:06.630
I mentioned earlier we operate
across 30-odd countries,

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:09.090
so the key thing
for us is-- what

00:12:09.090 --> 00:12:12.563
that means is we have 30
different regulators that

00:12:12.563 --> 00:12:14.730
manage financial institutions
across those countries

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:17.700
that we have to answer to, we
have to show compliance to,

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:20.130
we have to work within
the breadth of remit

00:12:20.130 --> 00:12:22.500
that they give us
within their country.

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:23.940
So we're not just
an organization

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:25.732
delivering financial
services in Australia,

00:12:25.732 --> 00:12:28.620
but because we work
across 30 countries,

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:30.060
we have 30 regulators
to work with

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:33.150
and we continually are
asking ourselves is there

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:36.030
a way that we can start
to demonstrate compliance

00:12:36.030 --> 00:12:37.860
in a different way
that would make

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:39.910
our lives a little bit easier?

00:12:39.910 --> 00:12:41.880
So I've talked a
little bit about what

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:44.140
ANZ is, what we're
trying to achieve,

00:12:44.140 --> 00:12:46.133
the reasons for what
we're trying to do.

00:12:46.133 --> 00:12:48.300
And what I'm going to do
is hand back over to Google

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:51.060
to see if they can give us some
insights and some products that

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:52.980
can help with that journey.

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:53.480
Juan.

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:55.330
JUAN SEBASTIAN OVIEDO:
Thank you, Rakesh.

00:12:55.330 --> 00:12:59.820
So let's take a moment to
remember a great product

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:02.160
experience that you
had in the past.

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:05.730
Remember the excitement you felt
as you were opening that box

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:08.640
and turning on that device,
or as you were signing up

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:10.500
for an account and
that new service,

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:13.560
or as you were being handed
the keys to that new car.

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:15.780
For you to get to
that point, you

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:18.930
had to make a decision to trust
that that product was going

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:20.720
to deliver on its promise.

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:22.680
And for you to continue
to use that product

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:24.900
and continue to get
the value from it,

00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:27.750
you had to continue to
believe that that trust was

00:13:27.750 --> 00:13:29.310
going to be fulfilled.

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:31.950
So, in essence, for customers
to have a great customer

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:33.990
experience, there
has to be trust

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:37.680
that the product will
deliver on what it promises.

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:41.820
And in order for your customers
to give your product a try,

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:43.890
they have to have
that trust, but also

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:46.590
for them to have the
potential to eventually become

00:13:46.590 --> 00:13:50.040
loyal customers, that trust
needs to be maintained.

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:54.370
And security is key to
maintaining that trust.

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:55.800
And that's why at
Google Cloud we

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:57.720
are focused on
giving you solutions

00:13:57.720 --> 00:13:59.760
that enable you
to apply security

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:02.130
while enabling your
teams to create and take

00:14:02.130 --> 00:14:05.910
advantage of the velocity
provided by containers.

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:08.520
So looking at the different
stages of the [? server ?]

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:11.430
supply chain, we provide
different solutions.

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:14.760
Starting with the Cloud
Build, our CI/CD platform,

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:17.550
you can use it to build
different kinds of source code

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:19.740
from different repositories.

00:14:19.740 --> 00:14:22.500
From there, you can make use
of the managed base images

00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:26.700
that I will go into
more detail soon.

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:29.220
Then as the different
artifacts are built

00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:31.800
and the container images are
pushed the Container Registry,

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:34.960
we scan them for known
security vulnerabilities.

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:37.830
This gives you the ability to
identify those issues early on

00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:40.650
and the opportunity to
address them, if needed.

00:14:40.650 --> 00:14:43.440
Then you can use
binary authorization

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:46.140
to define deployment
time policies to ensure

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:48.660
that only artifacts that
meet your organization's

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:51.510
requirements can be deployed.

00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:53.490
And then once your
service is running,

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:56.780
we continuously
analyze the artifacts

00:14:56.780 --> 00:15:00.250
and if there are any new
security issues uncovered,

00:15:00.250 --> 00:15:02.670
via known vulnerabilities,
we'll make this information

00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:04.300
available to you.

00:15:04.300 --> 00:15:07.080
So now let's go to detail
with some of these products

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:10.620
starting with Google
Managed Base Images.

00:15:10.620 --> 00:15:14.010
Managed Base Images are
maintained by us at Google.

00:15:14.010 --> 00:15:17.910
They are available for
Debian, Ubuntu, and CentOS

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:21.630
and can be downloaded or
obtained from our marketplace.

00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:25.030
They are scanned for known
security vulnerabilities using

00:15:25.030 --> 00:15:29.070
GCR vulnerability scanning and
we regularly patch these images

00:15:29.070 --> 00:15:30.810
and keep them up to date.

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:35.142
We use these images in our own
products such as App Engine

00:15:35.142 --> 00:15:37.350
and they are a great resource
that's available to you

00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:41.280
to start your development
process from a secure base.

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:44.420
Now, let's talk about Container
Registry Vulnerability

00:15:44.420 --> 00:15:46.560
Scanning.

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:49.380
Container Registry Vulnerability
Scanning checks the images

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:53.950
that you push to a registry for
known security vulnerabilities.

00:15:53.950 --> 00:15:58.320
It supports scanning of images
based on Ubuntu, Debian,

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:02.310
Alpine, RedHat, and CentOS.

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:05.430
We continuously ingest data
from the corresponding vendor

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:08.040
security feeds and if
any new vulnerabilities

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:10.500
are found that apply
to your images,

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:13.840
we make this information
available to you.

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:16.840
The product also has
integration with [? pops up ?]

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:20.930
notifications, which enables you
to automate certain processes.

00:16:20.930 --> 00:16:22.690
For example, you
could automatically

00:16:22.690 --> 00:16:24.640
notify certain
members of your team

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:26.470
when a new
vulnerability is found

00:16:26.470 --> 00:16:28.930
or you could
automatically file a bug

00:16:28.930 --> 00:16:31.760
when vulnerabilities are found.

00:16:31.760 --> 00:16:34.780
Now, let's talk about
Cloud Build for a moment.

00:16:34.780 --> 00:16:40.055
Cloud Build is our platform
provided CI/CD solution.

00:16:40.055 --> 00:16:42.430
With Cloud Build you can build
from different source code

00:16:42.430 --> 00:16:43.240
repositories.

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:45.500
You can build different
kinds of artifacts,

00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:48.640
such as Maven packages
and container images.

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:51.520
As I mentioned earlier,
as the images are built

00:16:51.520 --> 00:16:53.170
and they're pushed
to GCR, they're

00:16:53.170 --> 00:16:55.480
scanned for known
vulnerabilities.

00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:57.670
You can also set up
your corresponding tests

00:16:57.670 --> 00:16:59.720
as part of the
Cloud Build process.

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:03.160
And as the images and
different artifacts

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:05.641
are deployed to your
service, the whole experience

00:17:05.641 --> 00:17:07.099
is covered as part
of the platform.

00:17:07.099 --> 00:17:09.740
So it's a great resource
that's available to you.

00:17:09.740 --> 00:17:12.087
And now let's focus on
the deployment piece.

00:17:12.087 --> 00:17:14.170
And I'll hand it over to
Sandra so she can tell us

00:17:14.170 --> 00:17:16.390
how binary authorization
can help you apply security

00:17:16.390 --> 00:17:17.713
at that stage.

00:17:17.713 --> 00:17:18.880
SANDRA GUO: Thank you, Juan.

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:20.255
JUAN SEBASTIAN
OVIEDO: Thank you.

00:17:20.255 --> 00:17:22.470
SANDRA GUO: So
binary authorization.

00:17:22.470 --> 00:17:26.030
Binary authorization
is a deploy time policy

00:17:26.030 --> 00:17:27.770
enforcement [? tool ?] point.

00:17:27.770 --> 00:17:29.390
So what does it mean?

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:32.780
It integrates with
GKE deployment API

00:17:32.780 --> 00:17:35.090
and it examines every
deployment request

00:17:35.090 --> 00:17:37.700
that come through
the API to make sure

00:17:37.700 --> 00:17:41.060
that the image has
proper signature that

00:17:41.060 --> 00:17:44.600
meets your customer-defined
policy before it

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:47.710
can be admitted into a
production environment.

00:17:47.710 --> 00:17:49.280
The policy is configurable.

00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:52.870
You can define the
right policy that

00:17:52.870 --> 00:17:54.190
works for your organization.

00:17:54.190 --> 00:17:58.750
So it may mean that
something passes

00:17:58.750 --> 00:18:00.920
a particular security scan.

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:04.030
It may mean that images are
blessed by QA engineers.

00:18:04.030 --> 00:18:08.650
It may mean that image is viewed
by your trusted builder or all

00:18:08.650 --> 00:18:10.930
three above.

00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:13.720
The binary authorization
uses standard signature

00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:17.260
verification, so it can be
plugged into your existing

00:18:17.260 --> 00:18:23.950
CI/CD tools, if you don't choose
to use a GCD-provided products.

00:18:23.950 --> 00:18:27.580
It just means that you have to
put a signature on the image

00:18:27.580 --> 00:18:29.590
before it gets deployed.

00:18:29.590 --> 00:18:32.170
So the way it works is binary
authorization integrates was

00:18:32.170 --> 00:18:36.430
your CI/CD pipeline and record
a signature as the image

00:18:36.430 --> 00:18:40.120
passes through each of the
stages in your CI/CD pipeline.

00:18:40.120 --> 00:18:43.450
And that signature
signifies that the image

00:18:43.450 --> 00:18:46.900
has satisfied the requirement
for that particular stage.

00:18:46.900 --> 00:18:51.100
For example, the builder put
a signature on your image

00:18:51.100 --> 00:18:54.220
saying, I am the secure
builder, I built this image.

00:18:54.220 --> 00:18:57.910
Or the scanner would put
a signature on a image,

00:18:57.910 --> 00:19:02.020
basically means this image has
satisfied my scanning policy

00:19:02.020 --> 00:19:03.832
requirement.

00:19:03.832 --> 00:19:05.290
And the other times
the image comes

00:19:05.290 --> 00:19:08.170
to be deployed at deploy time.

00:19:08.170 --> 00:19:11.650
There are a number of
signatures already available

00:19:11.650 --> 00:19:13.220
for verification.

00:19:13.220 --> 00:19:15.950
Now, binary authorization takes
a look of those signatures,

00:19:15.950 --> 00:19:18.030
compare with the policies
that you've defined,

00:19:18.030 --> 00:19:19.910
and said, OK, this
is a good image.

00:19:19.910 --> 00:19:23.020
You set aside all
the requirements

00:19:23.020 --> 00:19:26.470
in addition to
just being deployed

00:19:26.470 --> 00:19:28.510
by a authorized person.

00:19:28.510 --> 00:19:32.140
So, yes, it's a two-level check.

00:19:32.140 --> 00:19:35.110
The right person's deploying
it, and the right content

00:19:35.110 --> 00:19:37.340
is being deployed.

00:19:37.340 --> 00:19:43.130
If a image does not have the
satisfactory signature on it,

00:19:43.130 --> 00:19:48.220
then binary authorization would
block it and record a audit log

00:19:48.220 --> 00:19:51.190
for future reviews.

00:19:51.190 --> 00:19:54.010
There are break glass
mechanisms available.

00:19:54.010 --> 00:19:56.920
So if your developer is trying
to push a change that does not

00:19:56.920 --> 00:20:00.040
need a policy, but needs
to be applied anyway,

00:20:00.040 --> 00:20:02.860
they can break glass and this
would generate a audit log

00:20:02.860 --> 00:20:07.300
as well for your security team
to review this incident later

00:20:07.300 --> 00:20:08.200
on.

00:20:08.200 --> 00:20:10.690
Some of the popular policies
to be applied at runtime,

00:20:10.690 --> 00:20:13.690
as I mentioned
earlier, is making

00:20:13.690 --> 00:20:17.320
sure the image is actually
built by my organization.

00:20:17.320 --> 00:20:21.310
Make sure that image has passed
security checks, QE checks,

00:20:21.310 --> 00:20:22.840
integration checks, and so on.

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:28.670
Or making sure that when
I deploy a third party

00:20:28.670 --> 00:20:33.230
image that I did not build,
it needs a explicitly

00:20:33.230 --> 00:20:37.440
set white list that is
managed by my security team.

00:20:37.440 --> 00:20:41.330
So we're releasing GA at
this year's Google Next.

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:44.150
So we're very happy that
our products is going

00:20:44.150 --> 00:20:45.645
to be generally available.

00:20:45.645 --> 00:20:47.770
There are a few new features
that we're pushing out

00:20:47.770 --> 00:20:48.680
with our GA release.

00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:51.280
One of those is a
dry-run feature.

00:20:51.280 --> 00:20:53.960
Dry-run, it means that
you're able to now set

00:20:53.960 --> 00:20:57.230
a policy in
non-enforcement mode,

00:20:57.230 --> 00:20:59.540
so that you can try
out new deployment

00:20:59.540 --> 00:21:04.760
policies without risking
production interruption.

00:21:04.760 --> 00:21:07.100
Again, we integrated
with Cloud Audit Log

00:21:07.100 --> 00:21:10.430
for any would-be-blocked
deployment attempt.

00:21:10.430 --> 00:21:12.232
And so we would
record it in Audit Log

00:21:12.232 --> 00:21:13.940
and can review that
later on to make sure

00:21:13.940 --> 00:21:15.890
that everything is
working fine before we

00:21:15.890 --> 00:21:17.630
turn on enforcement for real.

00:21:20.290 --> 00:21:22.540
And another new feature
that is coming out

00:21:22.540 --> 00:21:25.120
is system content policies.

00:21:25.120 --> 00:21:28.390
So when you are running
on GKE, there are a number

00:21:28.390 --> 00:21:31.990
of Google-owned system
containers that are running

00:21:31.990 --> 00:21:35.540
in your cluster as part of the
[? orchestration ?] system--

00:21:35.540 --> 00:21:39.160
things like containers used
for logging or monitoring,

00:21:39.160 --> 00:21:40.967
for example.

00:21:40.967 --> 00:21:42.550
So even though these
system containers

00:21:42.550 --> 00:21:46.240
are managed by
Google, users could

00:21:46.240 --> 00:21:49.660
deploy a alternative version
of the system containers

00:21:49.660 --> 00:21:51.880
to your cluster.

00:21:51.880 --> 00:21:56.000
And it's a attack surface.

00:21:56.000 --> 00:21:57.940
So how do we--

00:21:57.940 --> 00:21:59.830
before customers,
basically, have

00:21:59.830 --> 00:22:02.230
to make sure that,
yes, I'm deploying

00:22:02.230 --> 00:22:04.810
the right versions of the
system container-- making sure

00:22:04.810 --> 00:22:06.970
that those systems
containers are not being

00:22:06.970 --> 00:22:09.880
[? overwritten. ?] With
binary authorization GA,

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:12.710
we try to automate that for you.

00:22:12.710 --> 00:22:14.860
We have a default
global policy that we

00:22:14.860 --> 00:22:19.150
created by working
with the GKE release

00:22:19.150 --> 00:22:22.270
and build team to make sure
that every system container that

00:22:22.270 --> 00:22:25.830
is build by Google, that
build by GKE team is signed,

00:22:25.830 --> 00:22:30.510
and that we're able to
verify that signature for you

00:22:30.510 --> 00:22:33.940
at deploy time using this
global system container policy,

00:22:33.940 --> 00:22:35.930
if you opt in.

00:22:35.930 --> 00:22:38.290
So with this on top
of your existing

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:42.610
customer-defined policy and
third party image whitelisting,

00:22:42.610 --> 00:22:46.990
you will have your entire
cluster workload accounted for.

00:22:46.990 --> 00:22:52.380
So in your cluster all the
customer in-house viewed images

00:22:52.380 --> 00:22:54.200
will be signed by
you-- will have

00:22:54.200 --> 00:22:58.360
a signature requirement from
your customer-defined policy.

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:00.310
All the third party
images will be

00:23:00.310 --> 00:23:03.580
specified in the
image whitelist that

00:23:03.580 --> 00:23:06.840
can be managed by your
security team centrally.

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:08.800
All the Google-owned
system containers

00:23:08.800 --> 00:23:12.220
will be signed by
the GKE release team

00:23:12.220 --> 00:23:16.460
and verified at
deploy time as well.

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:21.820
Last, but not least, we also
have Cloud KMS support in beta.

00:23:21.820 --> 00:23:25.300
So now you can sign your
images using keys manage--

00:23:25.300 --> 00:23:28.790
asymmetric keys managed
in the Cloud KMS service.

00:23:28.790 --> 00:23:31.030
You don't have to
have the hassle

00:23:31.030 --> 00:23:34.880
of managing your own keys
anymore, if you don't want to.

00:23:34.880 --> 00:23:38.870
And along with this, we also
have generic PKCS key support.

00:23:38.870 --> 00:23:41.440
So if you have keys
managed elsewhere

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:44.590
in your preferred
PKI, you can use

00:23:44.590 --> 00:23:48.070
those with binary
authorization as well.

00:23:48.070 --> 00:23:51.070
So so far I talked about the
hosted panel authorization

00:23:51.070 --> 00:23:54.740
product and other vulnerability
scanning base image product

00:23:54.740 --> 00:23:56.930
that we host on
the GCP platform.

00:23:56.930 --> 00:24:00.070
But we also want to
create a open platform,

00:24:00.070 --> 00:24:05.500
and that is why we created
open source project Grafeas

00:24:05.500 --> 00:24:09.760
plus Kritis that are
open source reference

00:24:09.760 --> 00:24:14.140
implementations of the
container analysis metadata API

00:24:14.140 --> 00:24:15.760
and binary
authorization that are

00:24:15.760 --> 00:24:20.030
available for any Kubernetes
deployment anywhere.

00:24:20.030 --> 00:24:25.460
So we work with a number
of industry partners--

00:24:25.460 --> 00:24:28.860
JFrog, RedHat, IBM, Black
Duck, Twistlock, and more--

00:24:28.860 --> 00:24:32.220
to create the standard
metadata format

00:24:32.220 --> 00:24:36.630
so that they can be plugged into
our existing CI/CD pipeline.

00:24:36.630 --> 00:24:39.720
You may not have to use
the Google-provided product

00:24:39.720 --> 00:24:43.020
to get the benefit of
the secure framework.

00:24:43.020 --> 00:24:45.130
We want to create a
knowledge base for you--

00:24:45.130 --> 00:24:48.870
a metadata API, where you
can record findings and facts

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:50.790
about the containers
that it's going

00:24:50.790 --> 00:24:52.230
through your CI/CD
pipeline, that

00:24:52.230 --> 00:24:54.850
is going to run on your
security infrastructure--

00:24:54.850 --> 00:24:58.890
a knowledge base where you can
plug in metadatas, signatures,

00:24:58.890 --> 00:25:01.530
vulnerability findings that
you can later on browse

00:25:01.530 --> 00:25:04.230
through and use for
enforcement reasons

00:25:04.230 --> 00:25:08.010
or just for additional context.

00:25:08.010 --> 00:25:10.650
It plugs into the Kubernetes
admissions controller,

00:25:10.650 --> 00:25:12.750
so you have Kubernetes
deployment anywhere.

00:25:12.750 --> 00:25:16.245
You can use Kritis and it would
enforce signature-based policy

00:25:16.245 --> 00:25:20.730
or venerability-based policy
for you at deploy time.

00:25:20.730 --> 00:25:23.610
As I mentioned that we
have a number of support

00:25:23.610 --> 00:25:28.080
from partners and it's open
source built with community.

00:25:28.080 --> 00:25:29.970
Contributions are welcome.

00:25:29.970 --> 00:25:32.610
Check it out, if
you're interested.

00:25:32.610 --> 00:25:36.800
So with this, we hope to
create a open ecosystem

00:25:36.800 --> 00:25:41.918
where you can pick and choose
which component you want to--

00:25:41.918 --> 00:25:44.210
which vendor you want to use
for each of the component.

00:25:44.210 --> 00:25:48.950
You may have a different
build process using CircleCI

00:25:48.950 --> 00:25:53.480
or [INAUDIBLE] and you may have
a different scan process based

00:25:53.480 --> 00:25:55.820
on Black Duck or Twistlock.

00:25:55.820 --> 00:25:57.890
And you may choose to deploy--

00:25:57.890 --> 00:26:01.040
use Spinnaker or you
want to deploy manually.

00:26:01.040 --> 00:26:02.250
It does not matter.

00:26:02.250 --> 00:26:05.210
We want to create this general
framework where you can still

00:26:05.210 --> 00:26:09.020
record the metadata, the
signatures, and the findings

00:26:09.020 --> 00:26:12.470
in the database that you can
centrally access and manage.

00:26:12.470 --> 00:26:14.030
We still want to
give you the ability

00:26:14.030 --> 00:26:16.490
to control what goes into a
