00:28:47.330 --> 00:28:49.650
And then binary
authorization with Spinnaker

00:28:49.650 --> 00:28:53.020
will enforce that they are
available and present when

00:28:53.020 --> 00:28:54.270
we try and deploy a container.

00:28:54.270 --> 00:28:56.460
So as a container
progresses through this

00:28:56.460 --> 00:28:59.700
and gets to Spinnaker for
deployment, if it doesn't see

00:28:59.700 --> 00:29:02.310
all the attestations and
binary auth basically

00:29:02.310 --> 00:29:05.190
says that this does not fit with
your organizational policies

00:29:05.190 --> 00:29:07.930
that we have defined, it
will block the deployment.

00:29:07.930 --> 00:29:12.310
So, for us, the value add
here is a range of things.

00:29:12.310 --> 00:29:14.760
But, for us, it ensures
that the organization,

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:17.370
when it's deploying
containers into production

00:29:17.370 --> 00:29:20.580
and goes to our customers,
it's met a set of requirements

00:29:20.580 --> 00:29:26.380
that we are comfortable with
as a banking organization.

00:29:26.380 --> 00:29:28.630
So why use this capability.

00:29:28.630 --> 00:29:30.685
So I've already
referred to earlier on

00:29:30.685 --> 00:29:32.310
around the engineer's
experience, which

00:29:32.310 --> 00:29:34.190
